NAME

ulibLog - Writes logging information to the Xenolink log file(s)


SYNOPSIS

void  ulibLog(long Node, long EntryType, struct TimeDate *When, UBYTE *Data)
                  d0          d1                  a0                a1


DESCRIPTION

This function writes data to the Xenolink log file(s).

The <Node> parameter specifies which node's log file should be written to.

<EntryType> is actually a character promoted to a long integer, and specifies
the class of message.  Xenolink entry type conventions are:

   Entry type   Meaning
   ----------   -------
       |        Normal logging
       %        Errors (serious errors should be logged with ulibErrLog())
       +        Warnings
       @        Debugging information
       *        Logins and inbound and outbound connections
       <        Outgoing files
       >        Incoming files
       $        Accounting information (not currently used by Xenolink itself)
       S        Security information (not currently used by Xenolink itself)

The <When> parameter may either point to a (struct TimeDate) which specifies
the date that will accompany the logging information, or it may be set to NULL
(the more common approach) which will cause the current time and date to be
used.

Finally, <Data> is the string to write to the log file, which may be of any
length, although one should generally try not to exceed 80 columns in the log
files.

Any data written to a node's log will be simultaneously written to the global
CALL-MAIN.LOG, unless the Sysop has configured the system not to do so.


NOTES

Note that <Data> is only a simple text string, so if you wish to use %s and
%d, you will have to create a string of your own first; for example:

   sprintf(MyString, "Value is %ld", MyValue);
   ulibLogNow(XCBD->NodeNumber, (long) '|', NULL, MyString);


RETURNS

0 if the bit is not set.
1 if the bit is set.


SEE ALSO

